   "family": "mbarë",
   "is_root": true,
   "etymology": "First attested in 1555 by Buzuku as ëmbarë. Various theories exist on the etymology:\n* From mbar (“to bring (back), return”) (from Proto-Albanian *en-bara; further related to Albanian mbart (“to bear, carry”), mbaroj (“to finish, complete, end, fulfill, comply”), mbaj (“to carry, hold”)).\n* Orel disagrees with Meyer; proposed it as a tabooistically used loanword for Latin impar (“uneven, unequal”).\n* Finally, most likely, from an ën- compound, ën- + parë (“first, primary”), paralleled by mbrapë (“last, rear; bad, mean”), from ën- + prapë (“hind, backwards”).",
   "etymology_class": "inherited",
   "source_lang": null,
   "parents": [],
   "derived": [],
   "related": [],
